# Line endings: git stores LF, working copies get what the tool needs.
#
# WHY. On 2026-08-29 two abandoned clones of this repository showed 56 and 288
# "modified" files with identical insertion and deletion counts — 10042/10042
# and 49519/49519. Not a single real change: an editor had rewritten LF to CRLF,
# and git flagged every line as modified. `git diff --ignore-cr-at-eol` came back
# empty on both.
#
# Without this file the same ghost diffs return whenever a file is touched by a
# tool with different line-ending defaults — and real changes drown in the noise.
* text=auto

# Windows batch files MUST keep CRLF. cmd.exe reads them in byte-sized chunks
# looking for CR; with LF only, the split lands mid-character in multi-byte
# UTF-8 and the line tears in half. Invisible with ASCII — one byte, one char.
*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.ps1 text eol=crlf

# Shell scripts must keep LF even on Windows checkouts.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Binary: never diff, never merge, never touch line endings.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.webp binary
*.ico binary
*.pdf binary
*.zip binary
*.7z binary
*.gz binary
*.plugin binary
